76002.description:
Modern professional sport is high stakes, big business and is played out under the microscope of the media and general public’s intense and passionate gaze. As such, stakeholders across the sports sector clearly need the assistance of highly specialised and experienced legal advisors.

In this subject we will investigate the interaction between sport and the law, exploring issues including: 

• how sporting organisations mitigate against corruption risks, including those posed by gambling and doping; 
• how sporting bodies identify and resolve/mitigate against legal and commercial risks relating to their major revenue drivers (including media rights, sponsorship and events); 
• how disputes are adjudicated and resolved in a sporting context (including decisions of sporting bodies, on-field foul play and off-field misconduct); 
• how sporting organisations deal with claims of discrimination; 
• what legal principles are relevant to and constrain the actions of sporting bodies in their decision-making (e.g. restraint of trade); and 
• what are the emerging legal and integrity issues that sporting organisations are facing. 

The classes and assessments for this subject are designed to be practical and promote the development of the skills and tools required to succeed as legal counsel in this industry. In class, you will engage in discussions and collaborative activities to identify and explain the key principles of sports law, analyse how those principles operate, and apply these principles in resolving legal problems associated with sport. Guest presentations by current practitioners will help you evaluate the adequacy of sports law in dealing with emerging legal problems and understanding the perspectives of key stakeholders. The assessments are geared towards the development of your advocacy skills and ability to think critically and react in the moment, including through the use of mock negotiations and tribunal submissions.

76110.description:
This subject introduces you to the key concepts of public international law. You will explore topics including:  

• the sources of international law; 

• the relationship of international law to national law;  

• jurisdiction and immunities;  

• law of treaties;  
• State Responsibility; 

• settlement of international disputes; and  

• use of force 

Through small-group interactive learning activities focused on problem-based scenarios, you will review key concepts and develop your reasoning skills applying principles of public international law. You’ll refine your legal analysis and writing skills in preparing a case note and develop your analytical skills through the practical application of the rules of public international law to various problems.

79603.description:
This subject introduces you to the legal rules that affect traders doing business where an international element is involved. You’ll focus on legal and practical matters relating to: 

• the uniform law governing contracts for the international sale of goods (CISG); 
• the law relating to the international carriage of goods, including insurance and payment;
• exporting through various licensing and franchising arrangements; 
• the resolution of any disputes and enforcement of judgments; and
• the structure, procedures and work of the World Trade Organisation. 

You’ll learn to identify international rules applicable to various forms of commercial transactions. Through analysing hypothetical problems, you will apply international rules and case law to provide appropriate advice to a client in resolving international contractual disputes.
